Hehe - yes, I read the entirety of that thread when researching 
suggested new interfaces.

I can say that I am proposing implementing this myself (albeit not 
attempting to draw), I want it and don't use ASCII (can't be bothered to 
learn the ASCII symbols or check each ")" to see what it is), and, 
unlike Falcon's Eye, I'm proposing using the already existing and very 
powerful Qt system.

AFAIK noeGNUd (which I haven't actually used yet I must admit) was the 
work of one man, so the scale of what I propose is absolutely feasible. 
For the initial release, it should be possible to find existing SVG 
images of ASCII, so no artwork required until the coding is done.

I'm aiming for the following advantages over the existing Win32/Qt2/GTK2 
GUIs (all of which plus Falcon I have used):

# Nicer looks - SVG over bitmaps, my initial GUI brainwave

# Mousie interface (in addition to keys), lessens learning curve and 
hopefully faster to use

# Make typing error induced blunders much less likely, helped by mousie 
interface

I've no interest in coding for Win32 or GTK2 APIs, so I'm aiming for Qt. 

The existing Qt GUI is based on Qt2 so I'm thinking of starting afresh 
with Qt4 rather than expanding a current GUI.

wxWindows is another option, which afaik is less powerful but easier for 
small projects. Using Macromedia Flash may be possible but that is 
probably a silly idea. A Java front end to the Nethack binary may also 
be possible.


